# T-3205 — Stale-sidecar-code canary (PL-392 / G-069 detection for the notify rail)
# Source of truth: /opt/termlink/.context/cron/stale-sidecar-code-canary.crontab (git-tracked)
# Installed to:    /etc/cron.d/termlink-stale-sidecar-code-canary (USER field syntax — do NOT load into a user crontab)
#
# A long-lived detached process keeps executing the code it loaded. The sidecar
# supervisor deliberately never restarts a live sidecar — it only starts missing ones —
# so a shipped fix can sit DARK in production with every surface reporting healthy:
# process alive, heartbeat fresh, canary quiet, guard layer green, commit pushed.
# Measured in T-3204: three sidecars ran from Sep 22 against code written Sep 28.
#
# Nothing distinguishes "running" from "running the current code". T-2405 built this
# exact detector for push-wakers and scoped it there, which made the gap MORE expensive
# rather than less — that canary's green reads as coverage of a class it covers one
# member of. This is the sibling for sidecars.
#
# Empty log = healthy, per the convention of every canary in this tree. A firing entry
# means a live sidecar is executing superseded code.
#
# Operator action on firing — DELIBERATELY NOT AUTOMATED. Restarting a sidecar posts
# receipts visible to peers and can inject into live session prompts; T-3204's restart
# required explicit operator approval for exactly that reason. A canary that auto-fixed
# it would convert a visible staleness into a silent one.
#
#   pkill -TERM -f 'notify-sidecar[.]sh --agent-id'
#   bash scripts/notify-sidecar-supervisor.sh
#
# Then confirm the RUNNING code carries the change via an observable only the new code
# can produce. A fresh start time proves a restart, not which code it loaded (PL-392).
#
# Ad-hoc: bash scripts/check-stale-sidecar-code.sh
# Exit:   0 all current (or none found) · 1 stale · 2 tooling (fail-closed)
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Daily. The stderr split is load-bearing (T-2685): a tooling error must never dirty the
# findings log, because "empty log = healthy" is a one-bit channel and once it is dirty a
# genuine finding appends to an already-non-empty file and changes nothing an operator
# can see.
17 6 * * * root cd /opt/termlink && bash scripts/check-stale-sidecar-code.sh --quiet >> .context/working/.stale-sidecar-code-canary.log 2>> .context/working/.stale-sidecar-code-canary.log.stderr
